<p class="gna-lede">Facial swelling from a tooth infection is an emergency when it affects your breathing or swallowing, spreads into the floor of the mouth or neck, closes your eye, or comes with fever and difficulty opening your mouth. In those situations, go to the nearest emergency department or call 108 straight away. Even a swelling that seems mild needs to be seen by a dentist or maxillofacial surgeon within a day or two, because the infected tooth itself has to be treated — antibiotics alone rarely solve the problem.</p>

<h2 id="red-flags">Red-flag signs: when to go to emergency</h2>
<p>A dental abscess is a collection of pus caused by bacteria, usually at the root of a tooth whose nerve has died or in a deep gum pocket. Most stay small and localised. A few break out of the bone and spread through the soft tissues of the face and neck, where swelling can progress over hours. The warning signs below suggest that infection is spreading into deeper spaces or affecting the body as a whole.</p>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Go to an emergency department now, or call 108, if you have any of these:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>Difficulty or pain swallowing, or drooling because you cannot swallow your saliva</li>
    <li>Difficulty breathing, noisy breathing, or a muffled, "hot potato" voice</li>
    <li>Swelling under the tongue or in the floor of the mouth, or a tongue that feels pushed upwards</li>
    <li>Swelling spreading down into the neck or across both sides of the jaw</li>
    <li>Swelling that is closing your eye, pain on moving the eye, or any change in vision</li>
    <li>Fever, shivering, a racing heart, or feeling very unwell, confused or drowsy</li>
    <li>Being unable to open your mouth more than a finger or two (trismus)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Do not wait to see whether antibiotics start working, and do not try to drain the swelling yourself.</p>
</div>
<p>These signs matter because the tissue spaces beneath the tongue, under the jaw and around the throat are connected. Swelling there can push the tongue upwards and backwards and narrow the airway. An emergency team can protect the airway, start intravenous treatment and arrange urgent surgery if it is needed.</p>

<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Swelling with difficulty breathing, swallowing or opening the mouth, or a closing eye, is a medical emergency.</li>
    <li>The cause — usually an infected tooth — must be treated by draining pus and removing or root-treating the tooth.</li>
    <li>Antibiotics support treatment but rarely clear a dental abscess on their own.</li>
    <li>Diabetes, a weakened immune system and delay all raise the risk of serious spread.</li>
    <li>Even mild facial swelling from a tooth needs a dental or surgical review within one to two days.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="how-infection-spreads">How does a tooth infection spread into the face?</h2>
<p>When decay or a crack reaches the nerve of a tooth, bacteria enter the pulp and travel down to the root tip. The body walls this off as an abscess. If pressure builds, the pus follows the path of least resistance through the thin outer layer of the jawbone. Where it goes next depends largely on which tooth is involved:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Upper front teeth and canines</strong> can cause swelling of the upper lip, the side of the nose and the area below the eye.</li>
  <li><strong>Upper back teeth</strong> may cause cheek swelling and, occasionally, spread towards the eye socket.</li>
  <li><strong>Lower front teeth</strong> can cause swelling of the chin and the area just beneath it.</li>
  <li><strong>Lower molars and wisdom teeth</strong> cause the most concern. Their roots often extend below the muscle that forms the floor of the mouth, so infection can pass into the spaces under the jaw and tongue and down into the neck.</li>
</ul>
<p>Swelling that begins as a soft, tender puffiness and becomes hard, hot and spreading is called <strong>cellulitis</strong>. Infected gum around a partly erupted wisdom tooth (pericoronitis) is another common starting point — see our guide to <a href="/blogs/symptoms-of-impacted-wisdom-tooth/">impacted wisdom tooth symptoms</a>. Infection can also persist after an incomplete or unsuccessful root canal; our article on <a href="/blogs/symptoms-of-jaw-infection-after-the-root-canal/">jaw infection after root canal treatment</a> explains the signs.</p>
<p>Not all facial swelling is dental. A blocked or infected salivary gland, a skin infection, sinusitis, an injury or an allergic reaction can look similar. Rapid swelling of the lips and tongue, especially with itching, a rash or wheeze, may be an allergic reaction (angioedema) and also needs emergency care.</p>

<h2 id="ludwigs-angina">What is Ludwig's angina?</h2>
<p>Ludwig's angina is a rapidly spreading cellulitis of the floor of the mouth, involving the spaces beneath the tongue and under the jaw on both sides. In most cases it starts from an infected lower molar. The name is old: "angina" here refers to a choking sensation, not to the heart.</p>
<p>Typical features include a firm, "woody" swelling under the chin and jaw on both sides, a raised tongue, pain on swallowing, drooling, difficulty speaking, fever and — as it progresses — difficulty breathing. Unlike a simple abscess, there may be little obvious pus at first; the tissues are swollen with infection rather than containing a neat collection.</p>
<p>Ludwig's angina is a life-threatening airway emergency. Treatment takes place in hospital and focuses on three things: securing the airway (sometimes with a breathing tube placed while the patient is awake, or occasionally a surgical airway), high-dose intravenous antibiotics, and surgical drainage of the involved spaces with removal of the infected tooth. Outcomes are far better when treatment starts early, which is why speed matters so much.</p>

<h2 id="antibiotics-alone">Why antibiotics alone are not enough</h2>
<p>It is common — especially when a dental appointment feels hard to arrange — to take a course of antibiotics from a pharmacy and hope the swelling settles. Sometimes it does, briefly. But antibiotics have real limits in dental infection:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>They do not reach pus well.</strong> An abscess has a poor blood supply, so medicine circulating in the bloodstream cannot reach the bacteria inside it in adequate amounts.</li>
  <li><strong>They do not remove the source.</strong> The dead pulp inside the tooth, or an infected gum pocket, keeps re-seeding bacteria. When the course ends, the infection often returns — sometimes more severely.</li>
  <li><strong>They can mask spread.</strong> Partial treatment may reduce pain and fever while infection continues to track into deeper tissues.</li>
  <li><strong>Repeated, unsupervised courses</strong> add to antibiotic resistance and can cause side effects without lasting benefit.</li>
</ul>
<p>The principle surgeons follow is <strong>source control</strong>: drain the pus and deal with the tooth — through root canal treatment or extraction — and use antibiotics as support when infection is spreading or the patient is unwell. NHS guidance reflects the same approach: a dental abscess needs treatment by a dentist, and antibiotics are not routinely the answer on their own. Painkillers can ease symptoms while you wait for treatment, but they do not treat the infection.</p>

