<h2 id="higher-risk">Diabetes and other risk factors</h2>
<p>Some people are more likely to develop a severe, spreading infection and should seek care sooner, at a lower threshold:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Diabetes</strong>, especially when blood sugar is poorly controlled. High glucose impairs the white blood cells that fight infection and slows healing, while the infection itself pushes blood sugar higher — a cycle that can quickly make someone very unwell. Because diabetes is common in India and often undiagnosed, a blood sugar check is a routine part of assessing any serious facial infection.</li>
  <li><strong>A weakened immune system</strong>, for example from chemotherapy, long-term steroids, transplant medicines or untreated HIV.</li>
  <li><strong>Older age, pregnancy, kidney or liver disease, and heavy alcohol use.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Delay</strong> — the most avoidable risk of all. Swellings managed with home remedies or repeated antibiotic courses for a week or more are more likely to need hospital admission.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you have diabetes and notice a swollen face or gum, check your sugar levels more often, keep taking your usual medicines unless your doctor advises otherwise, and arrange to be seen the same day.</p>

<h2 id="emergency-or-urgent">Emergency, urgent or routine?</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>What you notice</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Any red-flag sign: breathing or swallowing difficulty, floor-of-mouth swelling, eye closing, high fever, unable to open your mouth</td><td>Emergency department now, or call 108</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cheek or jaw swelling that is spreading, with toothache, but you can breathe, swallow and open your mouth normally</td><td>Same-day dental or maxillofacial assessment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>A small gum boil near a tooth, with little or no facial swelling</td><td>Dental appointment within one to two days</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Swelling that settled with antibiotics, but the tooth has not been treated</td><td>Book treatment for the tooth — the infection is likely to return</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>This table is a general guide, not a diagnosis. If you are unsure which row applies to you, err on the side of being seen sooner.</p>

<h2 id="treatment-recovery">What treatment involves, and recovery</h2>
<p>Treatment depends on how far the infection has spread.</p>
<h3>Localised abscess</h3>
<p>The abscess is drained — through the tooth, through the gum, or by removing the tooth — under local anaesthesia. The tooth is then either root-canal treated or extracted. Many people feel noticeably better within a day or two of drainage.</p>
<h3>Spreading or deep-space infection</h3>
<p>Hospital admission is usually needed. Assessment includes blood tests, a blood sugar check and often a contrast CT scan of the face and neck to show which spaces are involved. Under general anaesthesia, the surgeon drains the infected spaces through incisions inside the mouth, under the jaw, or both, places small soft drains and removes the source tooth. Intravenous antibiotics, fluids and pain relief continue for a few days, and the drains are typically removed after one to three days as the discharge settles.</p>
<h3>Recovery and risks</h3>
<p>Recovery varies with the severity of the infection and your general health. After a hospital admission for a deep-space infection, many people go home within a few days and return to normal activities over one to two weeks. Mouth opening may stay stiff for a while and usually improves with gentle exercises.</p>
<p>Possible risks of surgery include a small scar where a drain was placed under the jaw, temporary weakness of the lower lip from irritation of a nerve branch, bleeding, and the general risks of anaesthesia. These are weighed against the far greater dangers of untreated spreading infection, which include airway obstruction, spread into the chest, sepsis and, around the eye, threats to vision.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>Your general dentist can treat most dental abscesses. Ask to be referred to an oral and maxillofacial surgeon if:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Swelling extends beyond the gum into the cheek, jaw, under the chin or around the eye</li>
  <li>Swelling returns after antibiotics or after a root canal</li>
  <li>The cause is a deeply impacted wisdom tooth</li>
  <li>You have diabetes or a weakened immune system and any facial swelling</li>
  <li>A swelling is not tender but is slowly enlarging — this may not be an infection at all, and could be a cyst (see our guide to <a href="/blogs/jaw-cyst-symptoms-types-treatment/">jaw cyst symptoms and types</a>)</li>
</ul>
<p>For swelling without red-flag signs, you can <a href="/book-appointment">book a consultation</a> at our clinic in Gachibowli, Hyderabad. Where admission is needed, surgery is carried out at NABH-accredited partner hospitals, and cashless insurance may be available subject to your policy terms and pre-authorisation. Read more about our <a href="/facial-swelling-treatment">facial swelling treatment</a>. For any red-flag sign, though, do not wait for an appointment — go to the nearest emergency department or call 108.</p>
